---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { siteConfig } from "../site-config";

// STARTER_CONTENT: replace the hero artwork and its alt text below, then
// remove this comment.
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A lecture theatre reduced to flat gold and black shapes, rows of seats sweeping past the frame, in a two-ink risograph print"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What this course is</h2>
  <p>
    Dota 2 has produced an enormous amount of teaching material and almost none
    of it is a course. Purge's guide is a reference. The wiki is a reference.
    Valve's in-game tutorial is a checklist you complete once and never open
    again. All of them are good at what they are, and not one of them has an
    order, a workload, or anything you have to hand in.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is the same material arranged into twelve weeks. Each week is built
    around one decision a new player actually runs into — should I hit this
    creep, when should I leave the lane, who goes first — and the mechanics
    are taught as the tools that answer that week's decision rather than as a
    list of things the game contains. One lens runs underneath all twelve:
    everything you do here spends something, and what you have to spend is
    gold, experience, time, space and information.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    People who have never played a MOBA. There are no prerequisites beyond
    owning the game and no assumption that you have played anything like it. If
    you are arriving from League of Legends, week 1 carries a short bridging
    passage on what transfers and what quietly does not, and after that you are
    with everyone else.
  </p>
  <p>
    You will lose a great deal, especially early, and the course is built on
    the assumption that you will. Losing is not the problem. Losing without
    being able to name the decision that cost you the game is the problem, and
    most of what you hand in is arranged around closing that gap.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>
        Twelve weeks, twelve decisions, in the order a player learns to make
        them rather than the order the game presents them.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>
        Four assignments and no exam, ending in a drafted best-of-three against
        another team from the course.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Policies" href="/policies/">
      <p>
        Teams and captains, the Hall of Fame that carries no marks, and the
        patch every figure on this site is pinned to.
      </p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
